.TH DE25-FPGA 8 "November 2026" "de25-nano-fpga-tools" "DE25-Nano"
.SH NAME
de25-fpga \- manage the configuration of the DE25-Nano's FPGA
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B de25-fpga status
.br
.B de25-fpga list
.br
.B de25-fpga select
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I name
.br
.B de25-fpga disable
.br
.B de25-fpga flash-write
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RI [ boot ]
.br
.B de25-fpga flash-restore
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The DE25-Nano's chip is configured in two phases, in what Altera calls HPS boot first mode.
.PP
Phase 1 happens at power-on, from the board's flash.
The flash holds the
.IR "HPS image" :
the settings of the SoC (its pins and memory, and which of its peripherals are routed to
the FPGA) and U-Boot's first stage, the SPL.
.PP
Phase 2 happens later, when U-Boot loads a
.I core image
into the FPGA from the SD card.
The core image is the FPGA's logic and pins.
.PP
Several cores can be installed side by side, each from a package named
.BI de25-nano-core- name\fR.
One of them, or none, is
.I selected
to be loaded at boot.
A core loads only on the HPS image it was built for, which comes in a
.I boot package
named
.BI de25-nano-boot- boot\fR,
together with the
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
that belong to it; each core names the boot package it needs.
If the chip refuses a core, Linux still starts, without it, but the board then cannot
restart until its power has been unplugged and plugged in again.
.PP
.B de25-fpga
keeps the two in step.
Selecting a core is the ordinary thing to do with it; it has the flash written when the
core needs that, after asking.
Installing or upgrading packages never writes the flash.
.PP
As the board is shipped, the flash holds Terasic's whole FPGA design, and the chip keeps
the flash from the SoC.
Linux cannot write it then, and U-Boot does: the HPS image is left on the card, and at the
next boot U-Boot checks it, saves what the flash held to the card as
.BI saved- crc32 .rpd\fR,
writes, and compares.
This takes about two minutes, and the LED beside the HPS KEY button shows how it is going:
one second on and one second off while it works, steady on when it has finished, four
blinks a second if it failed.
On a board whose flash already holds an HPS image, Linux writes the flash directly, and
what it held is not saved.
.PP
The chip takes up new flash contents only at power-on.
After the flash has been written, the board's power must be unplugged and plugged in
again; a restart is not enough, and must not be tried.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show what the flash holds, whether
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card are those of an installed boot package, whether a flash write is waiting or has
failed, the saved flash contents on the card, the selected core and the running core.
If a core was selected but is not running, say why, as far as can be told.
What the flash holds is shown only to root, who alone can read it.
.TP
.B list
List the installed cores, one a line: the name, whether it is selected and whether it is
running, and the boot package it needs.
.TP
.BI select " name"
Load the core
.I name
from the next boot on.
.IP
If the core can be seen to fit the flash, that is all.
It is taken to fit if the core now running needs the same boot package, if the flash
holds the HPS image of that boot package as installed, or if a flash write of that HPS
image is waiting for the next boot.
.IP
Otherwise the flash has to be written with the HPS image the core needs.
The command says so and asks; on a yes it writes the flash, or leaves it for U-Boot to
write at the next boot, puts the boot package's
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card, and puts the core in place.
On a no it changes nothing.
.TP
.B disable
Load no core from the next boot on.
.TP
.BR flash-write " [\fIboot\fR]"
Write the HPS image of the boot package
.I boot
to the flash, and put its
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card, without choosing a core.
.I boot
may be left out when one boot package is installed.
This is for bringing a board to a newer build of the boot package.
It says what it will do and asks first.
If the flash already holds exactly that HPS image, the flash is left alone and only the
two files on the card are renewed.
If the selected core does not load on the HPS image being written, it is deselected.
.TP
.BR flash-restore " [\fIfile\fR]"
Write saved flash contents back, from Linux, and stop a core being loaded at boot.
Without
.I file
it takes the saved file on the card, if there is exactly one.
The file must be the whole flash, 16 MiB.
With the factory contents back and Terasic's card in the board, the board is as it came.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Do not ask before writing the flash.
.SH UPGRADES
When the package of the selected core is upgraded, the new core image is put in place by
itself and is loaded at the next boot, as a new kernel is.
If the new version needs a different boot package, the card keeps the version it has, and
the upgrade says what to do.
Removing the package of the selected core has the effect of
.BR disable .
.PP
When a boot package is upgraded and the flash holds exactly the new package's HPS image,
.B u-boot.itb
and
.B boot.scr
on the card are renewed and take effect at the next boot.
If the HPS image has changed, nothing happens until
.BR flash-write .
.SH WITHOUT A CORE
Without a core the board has its serial console and Ethernet, with the Ethernet PTP
clock.
The signals on the GPIO headers, the HDMI output and the control of the fan come with
the core.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/core/ name /
A core:
.BR fpga.rbf ,
the core image;
.BR fpga.dtbo ,
its device tree overlay, which tells Linux what the core adds;
.BR boot ,
the name of the boot package it needs.
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/boot/ boot /
A boot package:
.BR hps.rpd ,
the HPS image, as raw bytes from the start of the flash;
.BR u-boot.itb ,
U-Boot with TF-A, which the SPL loads from the card;
.BR boot.scr ,
the script U-Boot runs, which writes the flash when asked and loads the selected core.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware
The card's FAT partition, which U-Boot uses:
.BR u-boot.itb ,
.BR boot.scr ,
the selected core as
.B fpga.rbf
with its name in
.BR fpga-core.txt ,
and
.BR u-boot-previous.itb ,
the U-Boot that was there before the last change.
.TP
.I /boot/dtbo/fpga.dtbo
The selected core's overlay, which the boot menu names.
U-Boot applies it only when the core image has loaded.
.TP
.I /dev/mtd0
The flash, when the chip has handed it to the SoC.
Not there with the factory contents.
.TP
.IR /boot/firmware/write-on-boot.rpd ", " /boot/firmware/write-on-boot.txt
A flash write waiting for the next boot: the new contents, and their checksum.
.TP
.BI /boot/firmware/saved- crc32 .rpd
What the flash held before U-Boot wrote it.
Keep a copy on another computer.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/write-on-boot-failed.txt
Left by U-Boot if the write failed: the step it failed in.
.TP
.I /proc/device-tree/chosen/de25-nano,core
The name of the running core, from its overlay.
.SH EXAMPLES
A new board, with its factory flash contents:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo de25-fpga select base
sudo reboot
.RE
.fi
.PP
The first command says that the flash will be written, and asks.
After the reboot, watch the LED; when it is steady on, unplug the power and plug it in
again.
.PP
Changing to another installed core that needs the same boot package:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo de25-fpga select tic
sudo reboot
.RE
.fi
.PP
Going back to the factory contents:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo de25-fpga flash-restore
sudo systemctl halt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Then unplug the power and plug it in again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR u-boot-update (8),
.BR flashcp (8)
